   432	
   433	`conformance.py:399–442,455–475` implements the signals and oracle flags. Market's
   434	406 variant is required alongside the generic variant (codebook §10.8,
   435	`../R1_triage_codebook.md:251–259`). Other SUTs' two variants are identical by
   436	construction and can be reported once. Empty checker output needs explicit
   437	counts/inspection, not an assumed pass.
   438	
   439	Fresh `attribute.py` gives NEW on a mapped operation **NEEDS_MANUAL**, NEW off the
   440	mapped operations **UNEXPLAINED**, and keeps baseline/mutant occurrence counts
   441	(`../tools/attribute.py:18–57`). It deliberately does **not** claim IMPL from a
   442	code manifest. PERSISTENT fingerprints can have increased occurrences; inspect
   443	these deltas too. Unexplained off-operation effects may be real state propagation,
   444	and a field mutation can merge with an existing fingerprint. Review exchanges,
   445	not just CSV totals (`PILOT_SUMMARY.md:84–85`; `OPERATORS.md`, shared model sites).
   446	
   447	Never use replay mode for E5: replaying the same saved exchanges against the
   448	unchanged spec cannot observe a new executable behavior. Replay's expected-label
   449	assignment would make accuracy circular (`attribute.py:1–7,33–39`;
   450	`PILOT_SUMMARY.md:97–100`). The manifest is the **injection intent** and reviewed
   451	edit provenance, not an independent diagnostic prediction or proof that every
   452	new failure is caused by that edit.
   453	
   454	The existing `triage.py` CLI is specific to P001 spreadsheet fault/message data
   455	(`../tools/triage.py`, main argparse block). It cannot directly consume these
   456	E5 violation JSONL files. No E5 adapter is implemented here. Use independent
   457	blinded source/log/request review under codebook §6/§10, or first design and
   458	validate a separate E5 adapter. Withhold site/operator/expected labels from the
   459	predictor/labellers until their outputs are frozen, then join against the
   460	manifest. Report pre-existing/BOTH/TEST cases separately; source location alone
   461	does not make a schema mismatch IMPL (codebook §5, §10.6–10.8).
   462	
   463	For each selected mutant retain: build-invalid; equivalent or oracle-blind;
   464	unexposed; causally killed; surviving exposed; flaky/inconclusive. Require the
   465	identical controlled request to pass on the control and fail on the mutant,
   466	with a trace or serialization witness that links the edit to the signal, before
   467	claiming a causal kill. A 2xx HTTP witness alone need not meet a response-schema
   468	oracle. Record any pre-existing diagnostic separately. Keep all ten selections
   469	in the denominator ledger and report eligible/compiled/exposed/killed counts
   470	separately, plus independent attribution precision/recall only over adjudicated
   471	causal failures. These are proposed evaluation rules [H], not measured E5
   472	results (`OPERATORS.md`, First E5 batch; codebook §5/§6).
    10	## Operator-level expectations
    11	
    12	All five inject on the IMPL side. The L1 expectation describes the newly inserted
    13	defect's intended origin; it does not relabel pre-existing SPEC/BOTH/TEST faults.
    14	Codebook: `08_experiments/R1_triage_codebook.md:90–114,228–259`;
