    19	|---|---|---|---|
    20	| DROP_VALIDATION | IMPL / I-ACCEPT-INVALID; I-CRASH if it instead crashes | INVALID_ACCEPTED for spec-invalid → 2xx; SERVER_ERROR for a causal 5xx | Revalidate the actual request with the unchanged spec; binding, service or persistence validation can still reject it. |
    21	| WRONG_STATUS | IMPL / I-RESP-CONTRACT | UNDOCUMENTED_STATUS, only without applicable exact/range/default response | A schema-free default can make the mutation invisible; do not pick 503 and then misinterpret ENV policy. These sites use 418. |
    22	| DROP_NULL_CHECK | IMPL / I-CRASH | SERVER_ERROR | Require an absence/null witness that reaches the removed guard. Existing crashes on the same path are not new ground truth. |
    23	| RESP_FIELD | IMPL / I-RESP-CONTRACT | RESPONSE_SCHEMA for a missing required field | Dropping an optional field is usually invisible. WRITE_ONLY targets serialization while retaining setters/input binding; verify framework behavior at runtime. |
    24	| REJECT_VALID | IMPL / I-REJECT-VALID | VALID_REJECTED, optionally UNDOCUMENTED_STATUS | Need a control 2xx for the identical request and equivalent state; spec-valid syntax alone does not prove resource existence or business validity. |
